# Dialogue economy

## Purpose

Keep the words on a page within what a panel can hold and a reader will read.
Text shares its space with the drawing. Every word past the budget covers art
or shrinks the type.

## Scope

Writing layer. Governs every lettering entry in `page.yaml`: balloons,
thoughts, whispers, shouts, radio, captions, narration, sound effects, and
signs. Placement and balloon craft belong to the `lettering/` rules.

## Core Principles

- The project sets the budgets. `project.lettering.max_words_per_balloon` and
  `max_words_per_panel` are limits. They are not targets to fill.
- The drawing carries the action. Words carry what cannot be drawn: speech,
  time, place, interior voice.
- A caption never explains what the drawing fails to show.
- One idea per balloon.
- A silent panel is a full panel.

## Word budgets

- No lettering entry exceeds `max_words_per_balloon`. Count the words of its
  `text` as written.
- The words of all lettering entries in a panel, added together, do not
  exceed `max_words_per_panel`.
- The sample project sets 25 and 50. A project that leaves the budgets unset
  SHOULD have them set at `/project-init`, and until then agents work to 25
  and 50 and say so in their report.
- The budgets are ceilings for the largest panels. A `small` or `inset` panel
  SHOULD carry well under half of the panel budget, and a `splash` gains
  nothing from being filled with captions.
- A sound effect SHOULD be one or two words.

## When a panel is over budget

Apply these in order, and stop at the first that works.

1. Cut. Remove what the drawing already shows and what the previous balloon
   already said.
2. Split the speech into two balloons in the same panel, if the panel budget
   allows.
3. Move part of the exchange to the next panel.
4. Add a panel. That is a layout change and goes back through `/page-plan`.

Shrinking the type, or letting a balloon cover a face or the action, is not
on the list.

## Captions and the drawing

- A caption MAY state time and place, carry narration or an interior voice,
  and bridge an ellipsis.
- A caption MUST NOT be used to repair a drawing: to say who is acting when
  the art leaves it unclear, to announce a handoff the panels never showed,
  or to assert a position the staging contradicts. Fix the panel plan instead
  (`comic-script-format.md`).
- A caption SHOULD NOT restate what the panel shows. "Mara climbs down the
  ladder" under a drawing of Mara climbing down the ladder spends words and
  adds nothing.
- Naming a character in dialogue helps the reader. It does not identify the
  character for the artist or the model, and it never settles an identity
  finding (`common/visual-evidence.md`).

## Dialogue

- Each line follows the speaker's `voice` in `character.yaml`. Mara uses
  short sentences and asks a question only when she needs the answer. A long
  speech from her is out of voice before it is over budget.
- Balloons are written in the order they are read. The first speaker in a
  panel is the one the staging puts first in the reading direction, so tails
  do not cross (`lettering/reading-order.md`).
- An exchange of more than two balloons per speaker in one panel SHOULD be
  spread over more panels.
- Dialogue compressed from the source keeps the author's words where it can
  (`adaptation-fidelity.md`).
- Emphasis is used sparingly. If every balloon shouts, none does.

## Validation

- `project.schema.json` defines both budgets as positive integers, and
  `page.schema.json` requires `text` on every lettering entry, so the count
  is arithmetic on two files.
- `docs/data-model.md` lists no ledger or review rule for the budgets. The
  letterer counts during the self-evaluation of `/panel-script` and
  `/lettering` and reports every entry and panel over budget.
- Whether a caption explains what the art should show is a judgment for the
  script-editor at the script gate.
- On the rendered page, the `lettering` check of the visual review covers
  text that hides the action, and `drawing_clarity` and `role_causality`
  catch the panels a caption was covering for.
